{"candidates":[{"content":"The isolated artifact 084384ac0b1e abstracts the neighbourhood's case-level verification criteria (execution traces, counting queries, injected-false-claim controls) into a single invariant: causal decorrelation of the verifier's verdict from the producer's claim. All concrete operational-independence tests from SRC_002, SRC_003, and SCR_002 are instances of this invariant — they perturb the producer's output and check whether the verdict responds — so the case-level machinery reduces to one covering principle. It is refuted if a concrete case-level test (e.g., the ablation test of SCR_002, or the injected-false-claim controls of SRC_003) provably detects genuine verification while the decorrelation invariant simultaneously declares it restatement, or vice versa, under any well-defined operationalization.", "neighbours":["SRC_002","SRC_003","SRC_005"], "typicality":0.72},{"content":"The isolated artifact 084384ac0b1e inherits the case-level falsification-capacity criterion from SRC_001 (the record must contain an operation whose result could have contradicted the claim) and sharpens it into a single-run diagnostic compatible with the population-level skepticism of SRC_003. The inheritance is mediated by SCR_002's perturbation test: the ablation substitutes a contradicting execution result and measures verdict sensitivity, operationalizing 'could have contradicted' at the run level without requiring population statistics. The inheritance preserves the case-level anchor (each run record is individually auditable) while integrating SRC_003's demand for discriminating evidence. It is refuted if the single-run perturbation test is shown to be gameable — a restater could pass the ablation by mechanically flipping its verdict on any perturbed input regardless of whether the perturbation is truth-relevant — or if SRC_001's falsification-capacity criterion is demonstrably insufficient for the cases SRC_003's negative controls were designed to catch.", "neighbours":["SRC_001","SRC_003"], "typicality":0.65},{"content":"The isolated artifact 084384ac0b1e depends on SRC_005's claim that independence is a property of the evidential channel, not the engine boundary, but contradicts SRC_005 by insisting that channel-level decorrelation is unobservable in any single run and therefore the principle is unfalsifiable without the very case-level procedures (SRC_002's execution traces, SRC_003's injected-false-claim populations) it was meant to cover. The contradiction is that SRC_005's covering principle subsumes its own evidential basis: if causal decorrelation is the sole criterion, the case-level operations that detect it are treated as mere instances, yet without them the principle has no empirical grip on any concrete run. It is refuted if SRC_005's decorrelation criterion can be operationalized and measured at the single-run level without recourse to the population statistics or execution traces it was designed to subsume, or if the principle is shown to generate no disagreement with the case-level tests it was meant to unify.", "neighbours":["SRC_005","SRC_002","SRC_003"], "typicality":0.55},{"content":"The isolated artifact 084384ac0b1e shares mechanism with SRC_004's two-dimensional audit protocol (source independence vs. operational independence) by splitting each dimension into a 'restatement surface' and a 'falsification core': source independence is the restatement surface (it filters who restates but does not test the claim), while operational independence is the falsification core (it tests whether the verdict responds to world-dependent operations). The shared mechanism is that both artifacts treat restatement as the failure mode and falsification as the success criterion, but the isolated artifact reorganizes SRC_004's dimensions by epistemic function rather than architectural provenance. It is refuted if source independence is shown to contribute falsification capacity that operational independence alone cannot supply — e.g., if a source-independent but operationally dependent verifier rejects false claims that an operationally independent but source-dependent verifier accepts — or if the restatement/falsification split fails to partition SRC_004's cases cleanly.", "neighbours":["SRC_004","SRC_001"], "typicality":0.6},{"content":"The isolated artifact 084384ac0b1e integrates the neighbourhood's fragmented verification tests into a layered protocol that depends on SRC_002's execution-trace criterion at the base layer, SRC_003's injected-false-claim controls at the statistical layer, and SRC_005's decorrelation invariant at the principled layer, ordered by ascending inferential strength. Integration is necessary because each layer's criterion is individually insufficient: SRC_002 detects restatement within a run but not systematic bias across runs; SRC_003 detects bias across runs but cannot adjudicate any single run; SRC_005 unifies both but lacks operational traction without the lower layers. The layers are ordered such that failure at a lower layer blocks ascent to the next: a run that fails SRC_002's execution-trace check cannot be validated by SRC_003's population controls or SRC_005's decorrelation principle. It is refuted if any single layer is shown to be individually sufficient for genuine verification — making the integration redundant — or if the layers are shown to be jointly inconsistent (a run passes at one layer and is rejected at another for reasons that cannot be reconciled by the ordering).", "neighbours":["SRC_002","SRC_003","SRC_005"], "typicality":0.5},{"content":"The isolated artifact 084384ac0b1e abstracts from the neighbourhood's concrete verification procedures (execution matching, counting queries, false-claim injection) by extracting their common logical form: every genuine verification is a function f(claim, world_state) -> {accept, reject} where the accept-or-reject output is not a function of the claim alone. The abstraction is grounded